<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Mozhaysk</h1><p class="st-meta">1389 CE – 1454 CE · capital Mozhaysk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A western appanage of Moscow whose prince Ivan helped capture and blind Grand Prince Vasily II during Moscow's civil war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moskva-river">Moskva</a> river west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, was taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1303. In his will of 1389 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> left it, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vereya">Vereya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belozersk">Beloozero</a>, to his son Andrei, who ruled it as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> approach from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>. During the long civil war over the Moscow throne, Andrei's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-andreyevich-of-mozhaysk">Ivan of Mozhaysk</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a>, and in 1446 he helped seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-lavra-of-st-sergius">Trinity Monastery</a>, after which Vasily was blinded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II</a> regained power, he sent an army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a> in 1454. Ivan fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and Mozhaysk was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> lands, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vereya">Vereya</a> continued for a time under Ivan's brother Mikhail.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1389 CE – 1454 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.34%;width:1.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1389 CE – 1454 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mozhaysk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andrei Dmitrievich, Ivan of Mozhaysk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mozhaisk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=principality-of-mozhaysk" aria-label="Principality of Mozhaysk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5467 284 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Mozhaysk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5787 540L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5787 540h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5787 540h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mozhaysk. <a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=principality-of-mozhaysk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figc